  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nTonight and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon features the persistent cutoff \nlow over the Pacific Northwest while broad ridging holds over the \neastern two thirds of the CONUS. A few shortwaves continue to spur \non convection across northern Minnesota and the Mid-Atlantic, while \nwe continue to see southwesterly flow aloft south of the main \nsurface front draped across the Dakotas towards southern Michigan. \nDewpoints have been over-performing over the last two nights, \nmaking the late afternoons/early evenings that much more humid,\nwith another warm night in store across the area. Lows across \nsoutheast Nebraska into southwest Iowa will struggle to fall \nbelow 75 degrees, while the coolest conditions only fall to \naround 70 degrees. Low-level winds have already ramped up just \nabove the surface, though convergence along the nose of the low-\nlevel jet will be pointed well to the northwest, limiting any \nchances for rain.\n\nHeading into Friday, another hot day is in store as the mid/upper \nridge holds over the area with no relief in sight. Afternoon \ndewpoints in the mid 70s will continue hanging around northeast\nNebraska into northern Iowa, while slightly lower values remain\nto the south where the hottest temperatures are anticipated to \nbe. A continued Heat Advisory will carry forward the message of \nthe dangerous heat -- not just due to the peaks of the heat, but\nalso the duration of the heat heading into the weekend. Heat \nindices are forecast to top out in the 100-106 degree range, \nwith the worst conditions along and south of Interstate 80. \n\nSaturday and Beyond:\n\nAnother warm early morning is expected to be interrupted by \nconvection initiating and being maintained by a nosing low-\nlevel jet across eastern South Dakota while additional weaker \nconvection tries to pull itself together across central Nebraska\nin worse convective ingredients. Once the strength of the LLJ \nbegins waning around sunrise, the weaker convection in central \nNE should die while the MCS/cluster across South Dakota tries \neating into the better instability to its immediate south. As \nit moves southward (and as time goes on), less and less shear \nwill be available to maintain its strength and the cluster \nshould fade as it pushes into northeast Nebraska and Iowa. \nLingering showers and pop-up storms will likely continue along \nthe outflow boundary throughout the afternoon northeast Nebraska\nand western Iowa, with storm strength expected to be low. \n\nAside from the shower and storm chances, heat is expected to \ncontinue as the main ridge across the area transitions from a center \nover the eastern half of the CONUS to a position over the western \nhalf. Temperatures take a slight dip for Saturday due to the \nstorm activity and resultant clouds, with temperatures expected \nto rebound into next week. Overall rain chances through the \nlatter part of the forecast continue to remain rather low, owing\nto the smaller-scale forcing for ascent that will occur, some \noff the nocturnal jet while others drift in from afternoon \nconvection that initiates along a weak mesoscale boundary. \nEnsemble models continue to lean towards the hot end of the \ntemperature forecast with us remaining planted on the eastern or\nwestern edge of a building ridge heading into next weekend. A \n\"cold\" front passage could bring in minor relief towards the \nmiddle of the upcoming week, but we'll still be left with above-\naverage temperatures that are still reminiscent of the deep \nsummer while most of the area continues looking forward to fall.",
